  "content": {
    "id": "p67-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "$P(x)=6x^3+x^2-5x-2$. Evaluate $P\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ and hence find the zeroes of $P(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=0$; zeroes: $-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{2},1$. Factorisation: $(3x+2)(2x+1)(x-1)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(-\\frac23\\right)&=6\\left(-\\frac23\\right)^3+\\left(-\\frac23\\right)^2-5\\left(-\\frac23\\right)-2\\\\&=-\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac49+\\frac{10}{3}-2\\\\&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $3x+2$ is a factor. Long division gives\n$$\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}&2x^2&-x&-1&\\\\\\hline 3x+2&6x^3&+x^2&-5x&-2\\\\&6x^3&+4x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&-3x^2&-5x&-2\\\\&&-3x^2&-2x&\\\\\\hline &&&-3x&-2\\\\&&&-3x&-2\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(3x+2)(2x^2-x-1)\\\\&=(3x+2)(2x+1)(x-1).\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the null factor law, the zeroes are $x=-\\frac23,-\\frac12,1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 75
  },
